Depth sensitivity is a weighting function (@cm-xps-depth: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @clean-manufacturing, #source "../sources/XPS-Attenuation-and-Information-Depth-Powell.lingua#r_0RF0DVE5MXB1BAG8S0W48X46TV", #clean-manufacturing "An-overlayer-attenuation-ratio-can-estimate-a-model-thickness.lingua#r_0Q2EDNA8Y1YT0BPVVGX6T17Z9G") { r_4HYZF57PBHJYF68FH8J99FYVYW
**Read:** [Attenuation and information depth in XPS — Cedric Powell](../sources/XPS-Attenuation-and-Information-Depth-Powell.lingua#r_0RF0DVE5MXB1BAG8S0W48X46TV), definitions, overlayer models and information depth. Revisit [A measurement observes a filtered field](../../../engineering/materials-engineering/research/A-measurement-observes-a-filtered-field.lingua#r_4P5XG26ENMZPFN5ED5HXZRWCV7).

For a deliberately simple flat, homogeneous medium with exponential attenuation, let ℓ = L cosθ, where θ is emission angle from the surface normal and L is an attenuation parameter appropriate to the model. A normalized depth weighting is w(z) = exp(−z/ℓ)/ℓ for z ≥ 0. Its mean depth is ℓ; the fraction of signal from above depth d is 1 − exp(−d/ℓ).

The depth containing fraction p is d_p = −ℓ ln(1 − p). At ℓ = 3 nm, the mean is 3 nm while the 95% information depth is about 8.98720 nm. Neither quantity says that all signal ends at that depth. A deeper region still contributes to the exponential tail.

The inelastic mean free path describes a material and electron-energy property. Effective attenuation length depends on the application and geometry used to account for scattering. Substituting a tabulated value intended for another application can invalidate the simplified inference. Real emission-depth distributions can depart from this single exponential.

**Work:** Derive the normalization, mean and information-depth formula by integration. Compare two emission angles under the simplified model, then identify effects that make the angle change more than a cosine rescaling. Explain which definition is needed for a claimed film thickness or depth-sensitive composition.

